<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Numerical Techniques</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A car traveling along a straight road is braking and its
        velocity is measured at several different points in time,
        as given in the following table.  Assume that <m>v</m> is
        continuous, always decreasing, and always decreasing at a
        decreasing rate, as is suggested by the data.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>seconds, <m>t</m></cell>
          <cell>0</cell>
          <cell>0.3</cell>
          <cell>0.6</cell>
          <cell>0.9</cell>
          <cell>1.2</cell>
          <cell>1.5</cell>
          <cell>1.8</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Velocity in ft/sec, <m>v(t)</m></cell>
          <cell>100</cell>
          <cell>99</cell>
          <cell>96</cell>
          <cell>90</cell>
          <cell>80</cell>
          <cell>50</cell>
          <cell>0</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Plot the given data below with time on the horizontal
          axis and the velocity on the vertical axis.
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
          <shortdescription>An empty coordinate plane for plotting</shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="18-dataplot">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/18-dataplot.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate the total distance traveled on <m>[0,1.8]</m>
          by computing <m>R_3</m>, <m>T_3</m>, and <m>M_3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="18">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              R_3 \amp = 96(0.6)+80(0.6)+0(0.6) \amp = 105.6
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              T_3 \amp = \frac{0.6}2\left(100 +
              2(96) + 2(80)+0\right) \amp = 135.6
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              M_3 \amp = 99(0.6)+90(0.6)+50(0.6) \amp = 143.4
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Given what you know about this situation, consider each
          method and determine whether it is an overestimate or an
          underestimate or whether you have enough information to
          determine that.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="18">
        <p>
          Because the velocity is decreasing, right endpoints will be
          an underestimate as will the trapezoidal rule.  It's unclear
          whether the midpoint rule is an over- or underestimate.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is your best estimate of the average velocity of
          the car on <m>[0,1.8]</m>?  Why?  What are the units on
          this quantity?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="18">
        <p>
          We'll use the midpoint rule to estimate the distance
          traveled.  Then
          <md>
            AV = \frac{143.4}{1.8} = 79.7
          </md>
          feet per second.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The rate at which water flows over a dam is measured in
        cubic feet per second (CFS).  As the floodgates are
        opened, the flow rate <m>r(t)</m> is given by
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>seconds, <m>t</m></cell>
          <cell>0</cell>
          <cell>10</cell>
          <cell>20</cell>
          <cell>30</cell>
          <cell>40</cell>
          <cell>50</cell>
          <cell>60</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>flow in CFS, <m>r(t)</m></cell>
          <cell>2000</cell>
          <cell>2100</cell>
          <cell>2400</cell>
          <cell>3000</cell>
          <cell>3900</cell>
          <cell>5100</cell>
          <cell>6500</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate the amount of water released using a midpoint
          approximation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="18">
        <p>
          <md>
            M_3 = 2100(20) + 3000(20) + 5100(20)=204,000
          </md>
          cubic feet.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate the amount of water released using the trapezoidal
          rule.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="18">
        <p>
          <md>
            T_3 =
            \frac{10}{2}\left[2000+2(2100)+2(2400)+
            2(3000)+2(3900)+2(5100)+6500\right] = 207,500
          </md>
          cubic feet.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate the average flow rate.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="18">
        <p>
          We will again use the midpoint rule to estimate
          <md>
            AV = \frac{204000}{60} = 3400
          </md>
          cubic feet per second.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Shown below is the layout of a fairway on a golf course
        where all the measurements are in feet.
      </p>
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>The measurements of a fairway on a golf course</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="18-golf">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/18-golf.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        A pound of fertilizer covers 200 square feet of grass.
        Estimate how much fertilizer is required for this fairway.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="18">
      <p>
        We first have to estimate the area so let's use the
        trapezoidal rule:
        <md>
          A \approx \frac{50}{2}\left[0 + 2(80)+2(85)+2(95)
          + 2(110) + 2(105) + 2(100) + 2(100) + 2(105) + 2(110)
          + 0\right] = 89,000
        </md>
        square feet.  So the amount of fertilizer is
        <md>
          \frac{89000}{200} = 445
        </md>
        pounds.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose you cut a fallen tree, whose length is 10 meters, at
        1-meter intervals.  Its cross sections are circle whose radii
        <m>r</m> at positions <m>x</m> meters from the end are given
        below.  Using the following data, apply the midpoint rule to
        estimate the volume of the tree.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>x</m></cell>
          <cell>0</cell>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell>5</cell>
          <cell>6</cell>
          <cell>7</cell>
          <cell>8</cell>
          <cell>9</cell>
          <cell>10</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>r</m></cell>
          <cell>0.68</cell>
          <cell>0.65</cell>
          <cell>0.64</cell>
          <cell>0.61</cell>
          <cell>0.58</cell>
          <cell>0.59</cell>
          <cell>0.53</cell>
          <cell>0.55</cell>
          <cell>0.52</cell>
          <cell>0.50</cell>
          <cell>0.48</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
    <solution component="18">
      <p>
        We have <m>V=\int_0^{10} \pi r^2~dx</m> so that the midpoint
        rule gives
        <md>
          V \approx \pi\left(0.65^2(2) + 0.61^2(2) +
          0.59^2(2)+0.55^2(2)+0.50^2(2)\right)
          = 10.65
        </md>
        cubic meters.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
